title: Event log
kind: overview
aliases: log history event-log
summary: Every write is a revision in a sequence of operations kept beside the data — so any change can be audited, undone, or reverted onto the current state.

Every change to a repository's metadata is recorded in its ''event log'', in the same
store and the same transaction as the change itself. The log is a ''sequence'' of
operations, each recording what it replaced, which is what makes it more than an audit
trail:

* ''auditing'' — the full history of any metarecord ([[Reading the log]]);
* ''undo'' and ''redo'' — take back the newest change ''you'' made, and take that back in turn;
* ''revert'' — undo one past revision or operation by writing what it replaced, onto the current state, as a new revision.

Nothing moves back over the log: it only grows, and loses history only by an explicit
prune, or by the retention limit (the last 200 revisions by default). It stopped being a
tree with a movable HEAD in October 2026 ([[Why the log stops being a tree]]); the rest of
that redesign — labels instead of revisions — is [[The operation log]].

